{"term":"progress","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:39a62e8dd0727bbd0b","definition":"Movement or advancement through a series of events, or points in time; development through time.","example":"Testing for the new antidote is currently in progress.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"usually","register":"uncountable, usually","wordFamily":["antiprogress","anti-progress","cross-progress","in progress","nonprogress","pilgrim's progress","practice makes progress","progress bar","progressist","progressless","progressophobe","progressor","progress report","royal progress","unprogress","work in progress"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/progress#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:dc4f390ff1372faa46","definition":"Specifically, advancement to a higher or more developed state; development, growth.","example":"Science has made extraordinary progress in the last fifty years.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"usually","register":"uncountable, usually","wordFamily":["antiprogress","anti-progress","cross-progress","in progress","nonprogress","pilgrim's progress","practice makes progress","progress bar","progressist","progressless","progressophobe","progressor","progress report","royal progress","unprogress","work in progress"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/progress#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:bfe66d85001af8671f","definition":"An official journey made by a monarch or other high personage; a state journey, a circuit.","example":"... Queen Elizabeth in one of her progresses, stopping at Crawley to breakfast, was so delighted with some remarkably fine Hampshire beer which was then presented to her by the Crawley of the day (a handsome gentleman with a trim beard and a good leg), th